**Implementing Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Vulnerability in SVEP Discussion Forums**

**Scenario:** A student character, "Alice," attempts to inject malicious JavaScript code into the discussion forum by posting a comment containing a malicious HTML string. This example demonstrates how an XSS vulnerability can be exploited and how to prevent it.

**Code Structure:**

The following code snippets are designed to illustrate the vulnerability and detection/prevention strategies. Please note that these examples are for educational purposes only and should not be used in a real-world application.

**Step 1: Create a Vulnerable Discussion Forum**

```html
<!-- index.html (discussion forum page) -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>Discussion Forum</title>
	<script src="script.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
	<h1>Discussion Forum</h1>
	<input id="comment" type="text" placeholder="Post a comment">
	<button id="post-btn">Post Comment</button>
	<div id="comments"></div>
	<script>
		function postComment() {
			var comment = document.getElementById("comment").value;
			var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
			xhr.open("POST", "/post-comment", true);
			xhr.setRequestHeader("Content-Type", "application/x-www-form-urlencoded");
			xhr.send("comment=" + comment);
		}
		document.getElementById("post-btn").addEventListener("click", postComment);
	</script>
</body>
</html>
```

```javascript
// script.js (JavaScript code for discussion forum)
function processComment(comment) {
	// This function is vulnerable to XSS attacks
	var commentElement = document.createElement("div");
	commentElement.innerHTML = comment;
	document.getElementById("comments").appendChild(commentElement);
}
```

**Step 2: Inject Malicious JavaScript Code**

```javascript
// malicious-comment.js (malicious code injected by Alice)
function evilCode() {
	// This code will be executed when the comment is rendered
	alert("You have been pwned!");
	var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
	xhr.open("GET", "/steal-data", true);
	xhr.send();
}
```

**Step 3: Alice Posts the Malicious Comment**

Alice submits the following comment to the discussion forum:

```html
"<script>alert('You have been alerted!');</script> This is a harmless comment."
```

However, the comment contains the malicious JavaScript code:

```javascript
"<script>evilCode();</script>"
```

**Step 4: Detection and Prevention**

To prevent XSS attacks, we need to sanitize user input and prevent malicious code from being executed. We can use a library like DOMPurify to sanitize the comment before rendering it.

```javascript
// sanitized-comment.js (sanitized code)
function sanitizeComment(comment) {
	return DOMPurify.sanitize(comment);
}
```

**Step 5: Update the Discussion Forum Code**

```javascript
// updated-script.js (updated JavaScript code for discussion forum)
function processComment(comment) {
	var sanitizedComment = sanitizeComment(comment);
	var commentElement = document.createElement("div");
	commentElement.innerHTML = sanitizedComment;
	document.getElementById("comments").appendChild(commentElement);
}
```

**Step 6: Prevent Malicious Code Execution**

To prevent the malicious code from being executed, we need to add an event listener to the comment element and prevent the default behavior of the script tag.

```javascript
// updated-script.js (continued)
commentElement.addEventListener("DOMNodeInserted", function(event) {
	if (event.target.tagName === "SCRIPT") {
		event.target.parentNode.removeChild(event.target);
	}
});
```

By following these steps and using DOMPurify to sanitize user input, we can prevent XSS attacks and ensure that the discussion forum remains secure.

